
  Those four have no DOM in them and no framework in them. The approaches differ only in the shell around them.

  [1mA. No build step — ES modules, test runner only.[0m [38;5;153mindex.html[39m loads plain [38;5;153m.js[39m modules; any static server runs it. Vitest
  tests the four core modules in Node with a fake clock; the DOM layer stays thin and hand-verified.
  - Buys: the prototype stays readable end to end, dependencies near zero, and the genuinely risky logic (time
    transitions, reload reconciliation) is still under test.
  - Costs: DOM rendering untested, no HMR, and it needs a local static server — ES modules won't load over [38;5;153mfile://[39m.
  - Rules out: nothing structural. Adding Vite later is a config file, not a rewrite.

  [1mB. Vite + Vitest + jsdom, still vanilla.[0m Same modules, plus dev server, HMR, and DOM-level tests.
  - Buys: the render layer becomes testable too, and you get the on-ramp if this grows into the real app.
  - Costs: at this size you'd have more config than code, and [38;5;153mnode_modules[39m in a prototype you described as
    throwaway-ish.

  [1mC. Preact + Vite.[0m A component model for the bell, badge, panel, and list.
  - Buys: unread-count rerendering and panel state come free instead of being hand-wired.
  - Costs: heaviest dependency surface, and it only buys the render layer — the interesting 80% of this design is
    framework-independent either way.

  [1mMy recommendation: A.[0m The hard part of this feature is time behaviour — firing once and only once per transition, and
  reconciling everything that came due while the tab was closed. That logic is pure, so a test runner with a fake clock
  covers it completely, and no amount of framework helps with it. Three views don't justify a component model. If this
  outgrows A, B is an additive step, not a redo.
